{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"concepts","title":"Concepts & Constraints (C++20)","group":"Templates & Generic Programming","order":47,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A concept is a named, compile-time predicate on types. Constraining a template with one gives clear error messages and lets you overload on what a type can do.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-check\"></i>Using standard concepts</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;concepts&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;std::integral T&gt;                 // T must be an integer type\nT gcd(T a, T b) { return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b); }\n\nauto half(std::floating_point auto x) { return x / 2; }      // abbreviated syntax\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\n    requires std::totally_ordered&lt;T&gt;       // requires-clause\nconst T&amp; clampTo(const T&amp; v, const T&amp; lo, const T&amp; hi) { return v &lt; lo ? lo : hi &lt; v ? hi : v; }\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; gcd(48, 18) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; half(5.0) &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; clampTo(15, 0, 10) &lt;&lt; '\\n';\n    // gcd(4.5, 1.5);   // error: constraints not satisfied — 'double' is not integral\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>6 2.5 10</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — constraint violated</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;concepts&gt;\ntemplate &lt;std::integral T&gt;\nT gcd(T a, T b) { return b == 0 ? a : gcd(b, a % b); }\nint main() { return static_cast&lt;int&gt;(gcd(4.5, 1.5)); }</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Compiler error — short and to the point</figcaption><pre><code>error: no matching function for call to 'gcd'\nnote: candidate template ignored: constraints not satisfied [with T = double]\nnote: because 'double' does not satisfy 'integral'</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-puzzle\"></i>Defining a concept</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;concepts&gt;\n#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nconcept Shape = requires(const T&amp; s) {\n    { s.area() } -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;double&gt;;    // must have area() returning something double-like\n    { s.name() } -&gt; std::convertible_to&lt;std::string&gt;;\n};\n\nstruct Square { double side; double area() const { return side * side; } std::string name() const { return \"square\"; } };\nstruct Blob   { int size; };\n\nvoid report(const Shape auto&amp; s) { std::cout &lt;&lt; s.name() &lt;&lt; \": \" &lt;&lt; s.area() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nconcept Container = requires(T c) { c.begin(); c.end(); c.size(); };\n\nvoid info(const Container auto&amp; c) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"container of \" &lt;&lt; c.size() &lt;&lt; '\\n'; }\nvoid info(const auto&amp;)            { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"not a container\\n\"; }   // less constrained fallback\n\nint main() {\n    report(Square{3});\n    // report(Blob{1});   // error: Blob does not satisfy Shape\n    info(std::vector&lt;int&gt;{1, 2});\n    info(42);\n    std::cout &lt;&lt; Shape&lt;Square&gt; &lt;&lt; Shape&lt;Blob&gt; &lt;&lt; '\\n';     // concepts are bool constants\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>square: 9\ncontainer of 2\nnot a container\n10</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept (<code>&lt;concepts&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;iterator&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;ranges&gt;</code>)</th><th>Satisfied when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>std::integral</code>, <code>std::floating_point</code></td><td>Built-in integer / floating type</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::same_as&lt;T, U&gt;</code>, <code>std::convertible_to&lt;From, To&gt;</code></td><td>Type relationships</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::equality_comparable</code>, <code>std::totally_ordered</code></td><td><code>==</code> / <code>&lt;</code> etc. work</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::copyable</code>, <code>std::movable</code>, <code>std::default_initializable</code></td><td>Object capabilities</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::invocable&lt;F, Args...&gt;</code>, <code>std::predicate&lt;F, Args...&gt;</code></td><td>Callable with those arguments</td></tr><tr><td><code>std::ranges::range</code>, <code>std::random_access_iterator</code></td><td>Range / iterator categories</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Four ways to write a constraint</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++ — equivalent forms</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;concepts&gt;\ntemplate &lt;std::integral T&gt; T a(T x) { return x; }                  // 1. constrained parameter\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt; requires std::integral&lt;T&gt; T b(T x) { return x; }   // 2. requires-clause\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt; T c(T x) requires std::integral&lt;T&gt; { return x; }   // 3. trailing requires\nstd::integral auto d(std::integral auto x) { return x; }           // 4. abbreviated</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What problem do concepts solve compared with plain templates?</summary><div><p>Plain templates accept any type and fail deep inside the implementation with pages of errors. Concepts state requirements in the interface: misuse is rejected at the call site with a short message, overloads can be selected by capability (more-constrained wins), and the code documents itself. They replace most <code>enable_if</code>/SFINAE tricks.</p></div></details></section>"}